  <h1>India’s Water Crisis — Scarcity, Management, and Policy Imperatives</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India is facing an escalating water crisis, marked by depleting groundwater, erratic rainfall, and rising demand. In 2024, The Hindu highlighted water shortages across Maharashtra, Karnataka, and Delhi, underscoring governance and equity concerns. For UPSC, water is a classic example of the intersection of environment, economy, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Per Capita Availability: ~1,100 cubic meters/year (water-stressed threshold: 1,700).<br/>•	Groundwater Dependence: ~62% of irrigation and 85% of drinking water needs.<br/>•	Overexploited Blocks: >1,000 blocks identified by CGWB.<br/>•	Urban Stress: Cities like Chennai, Bengaluru face “Day Zero” risks.<br/>•	Climate Factor: Erratic monsoons increasing floods and droughts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Groundwater Depletion: Punjab, Haryana, and western UP over-extraction.<br/>2.	Inefficient Irrigation: Flood irrigation, low micro-irrigation adoption.<br/>3.	Pollution: Industrial discharge, untreated sewage contaminating rivers.<br/>4.	Inter-State Disputes: Cauvery, Krishna, Ravi-Beas conflicts.<br/>5.	Urban Overuse: Rising demand in cities, leakage losses ~30–40%.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	Jal Jeevan Mission (2019): Household tap water connections; ~75% coverage by 2024.<br/>•	Atal Bhujal Yojana: Community groundwater management.<br/>•	Namami Gange Mission: River cleaning and rejuvenation.<br/>•	PM Krishi Sinchai Yojana: Micro-irrigation push.<br/>•	National Water Policy (2012): Integrated water resource management.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	Tap Water Expansion: Rural households with tap water rose from 17% (2019) to 75% (2024).<br/>•	Awareness Campaigns: Growing consciousness about water conservation.<br/>•	Technological Tools: Remote sensing and IoT-based monitoring in some states.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Gaps<br/>•	Funding Constraints: State finances stretched.<br/>•	Coordination Failures: Fragmented management across ministries.<br/>•	Equity Gaps: Dalit, Adivasi, and women face water access inequities.<br/>•	Climate Resilience: Adaptation strategies still weak.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Water critical for agriculture and industry.<br/>•	Health: Clean water reduces disease burden.<br/>•	Gender Equality: Women spend hours fetching water in rural areas.<br/>•	National Security: Water disputes could trigger conflicts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Geography): Resources and scarcity.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Water policy, Centre–State issues.<br/>•	GS-III (Environment & Economy): Irrigation, climate adaptation.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Water is the elixir of life, yet India is running dry.”<br/>o	“Managing water is managing India’s future.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Resource politics and federalism.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Demand-Side Management: Water pricing, conservation incentives.<br/>2.	Micro-Irrigation: Expand drip and sprinkler adoption.<br/>3.	River Basin Approach: Integrated, cooperative federalism.<br/>4.	Reuse & Recycling: Urban wastewater treatment and reuse.<br/>5.	Community Engagement: Water literacy and participatory governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s water crisis is both a developmental and ecological challenge. Sustainable management, equity, and cooperation are key to ensuring water security. For UPSC aspirants, it demonstrates how governance, climate change, and resource management converge in policymaking.</p>
